{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Comment sync (append-only) with persistent Jira-comment-ID map + rich rendering\n\n## Context / Problem\nComment sync currently identifies comments by **body equality** (a normalized-text\nkey computed in `outbound_comments._diff_comments`), which is fragile: two comments\nwith the same text collide, and an edited body looks like a brand-new comment. The\nin-code note at `outbound_comments.py` (`\"PR #402 … ID-based binding once available;\nthis body-equality match is the baseline\"`) already anticipates the fix. The team\nhas settled comment semantics as **append-only** — mirror comment *creation* both\nways, but do not sync edits or deletes — which keeps the design simple and matches\nthe reconciler's existing inbound behavior of skipping already-known Jira comment IDs.\n\nThis story ALSO **owns the DC comment-diff dedup-key migration that `3388`\ndeliberately deferred here.** `3388` redefines `WikiTextCodec.normalize_outbound`\nfrom identity to the rendering form for descriptions, but keeps the comment-diff\npath injecting the plain/identity normalizer UNCHANGED (via the a32a `_resolve_codec`\nseam, defaulting to `_IdentityCodec`) so the description cutover does not shift the\ncomment dedup key. Flipping the comment path to the real rendering codec — and\nkeeping comment-diff convergent when it renders rich — is this story's job, not\n`3388`'s.\n\n## Approach\nTwo coupled changes, both append-only and echo-safe:\n\n1. **Stable identity by a local comment key + the returned Jira ID, not body.** Each\n   rebar comment originates from a COMMENT event whose **HLC `timestamp`** (unique,\n   monotonic; already threaded onto the compiled comment entry at\n   `reducer/_processors.py:349`, alongside `author`) is the **stable local comment\n   identity** — nothing else on the entry (`body`/`author`/`source_*`) is\n   collision-proof once body-equality is abandoned. Maintain a **persistent map\n   `local_comment_key (HLC) → Jira comment ID`** in the binding state. Outbound: when\n   rebar has a comment whose local key has no mapped Jira ID, create it in Jira and\n   record the returned Jira comment ID against that local key. Inbound: mirror Jira\n   comments whose IDs are not already values in the map as new rebar comments (whose\n   entries carry the existing `jira_comment_id`, bug 85a1 `_processors.py:354-356`,\n   the inbound half of this identity). **Append-only**: never push rebar edits/deletes\n   to Jira comments and never apply Jira comment edits/deletes to rebar — an\n   already-mapped key/ID is never re-synced (the ID map is the durable echo guard for\n   comments, superseding the body-key match and complementing the existing\n   `RECONCILER_MARKER` machine-comment guard, `outbound_comments.py:72/177-184`,\n   which still excludes bridge-internal payloads). The ID map is change-gated and\n   fixed-shape (one small entry per comment, added once) to respect epic `0303`'s\n   churn discipline.\n1a. **Capture the returned Jira comment ID at the enactment sites (the write-back\n   data path), carrying the local key through the mutation.** The Jira comment ID is\n   only obtainable as `SupportsComments.add_comment`'s return value — a\n   `dict[str, Any]` carrying `{\"id\": …}` (`_backend.py:491`; both transports return\n   this shape — `adapters/jira/backend.py:265` Cloud,\n   `adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:433` DC), but **both enacting sites currently\n   DISCARD it**: `dispatch_one.py:418` (create path) and\n   `dispatch_apply_phases.py:165` (update path). Two links make the capture possible\n   and must be built:\n   (i) **the local key must ride ON the mutation entry** — the outbound builders\n   `_map_comments_for_create` (`outbound_comments.py:148`) and the `_diff_comments`\n   append (`:147`) today emit `{\"action\":\"add\",\"body\":…}` with no key; this story adds\n   `\"local_comment_key\": <HLC>` so the enactment loop knows which local comment a\n   returned id belongs to;\n   (ii) **the binding-store handle must reach both loops — and the update loop needs\n   the WHOLE call chain threaded, not just its leaf.** The create loop is already\n   wired: `_create_one` receives `binding_store` (`dispatch_one.py:154`), so it can\n   call `binding_store.record_comment_id(local_comment_key, returned[\"id\"])` directly.\n   The update loop is NOT: `_update_one_dispatch_comments`\n   (`dispatch_apply_phases.py:147`) has no handle, and neither does its caller\n   `update_one` (`dispatch_one.py:448`) nor `update_one`'s two production callers.\n   So this story threads `binding_store` down the full chain:\n   `handle_update` (`apply_handlers.py:201`) — which ALREADY holds the handle as\n   `ctx.binding_store` (`BatchApplyContext.binding_store`, `apply_handlers.py:81`,\n   already passed to `handle_create` at `:150`) — passes it into a new optional\n   `binding_store` parameter on `update_one` (`dispatch_one.py:448`), which forwards\n   it to `_update_one_dispatch_comments`. Production outbound updates route through\n   this BATCH path (`applier._apply_batch` builds the `BatchApplyContext`;\n   `applier.py` comment: \"outbound/untyped go to the legacy `_apply_batch`\"). The\n   second, typed-leaf caller `_apply_outbound_update` (`apply_outbound.py:159`, def\n   `:126`) delegates to the same `update_one`; it is threaded for parity by adding an\n   optional `binding_store` param forwarded from `_apply_typed`\n   (`applier.py:447`, which already receives `binding_store`) and passed through to\n   `update_one`. `update_one`'s param defaults to `None`, so every other caller and\n   all existing tests are unaffected.\n   The id-key extraction (`returned[\"id\"]`) is confirmed uniform across the Cloud\n   (acli) and DC transports. Failures stay non-fatal exactly as today (the existing\n   `comment_errors` capture is unchanged); only a successful create records a mapping.\n1b. **Two identity stores, one authoritative per DIRECTION — disjoint, never\n   consulted across directions.** The repo ALREADY stores inbound-origin identity as\n   a per-comment `jira_comment_id` field on the compiled entry (bug 85a1,\n   `reducer/_processors.py:354-356`, stamped when a Jira comment is mirrored inbound).\n   This story does NOT move or duplicate that: the existing per-entry\n   `jira_comment_id` stays authoritative for INBOUND-origin comments, and the new\n   binding-state map `local_comment_key → jira_id` is authoritative for\n   OUTBOUND-origin (rebar-created) comments. **The two never need to consult each\n   other, because each direction already has its own loop-breaker:**\n   - **Outbound direction** — the OUTBOUND differ (`outbound_comments.py`) keys on the\n     binding map (via the local comment key) so a rebar comment already mapped to a\n     Jira id is never re-created. This is the map's ONLY consumer.\n   - **Inbound direction** — the INBOUND differ `_diff_comments_inbound`\n     (`inbound_differ.py:280`) is left UNCHANGED and does NOT receive the outbound map.\n     It already refuses to re-import our own outbound comments two ways: (a) every\n     outbound body is decorated with `RECONCILER_MARKER` (`outbound_comments.py:178`),\n     and the inbound differ skips any Jira comment whose body contains that marker\n     (`inbound_differ.py:337`, \"outbound echo — do not pull our own comment back in\");\n     (b) it already skips any Jira id matching a local `jira_comment_id`\n     (`inbound_differ.py:299,319-321`), which covers inbound-origin comments.\n   So the map is authoritative for the OUTBOUND direction ONLY; the inbound path needs\n   no handle to it, and there is no cross-direction competition. This replaces the\n   fragile outbound body-equality membership test (`outbound_comments.py:143`) with ID\n   identity while leaving BOTH the inbound differ and the inbound `jira_comment_id`\n   field as-is.\n2. **Render comment bodies through the codec — adding the encode step the comment\n   send path currently lacks — and migrate the dedup key.** Outbound comment bodies\n   are rendered through the SAME `RichTextCodec.to_wire` seam the descriptions use\n   (`adapters/jira_family/rich_text.py` — `AdfCodec.to_wire` for Cloud, the\n   now-rendering `WikiTextCodec.to_wire` for DC after `3388`), applied in\n   `_map_comments_for_create`/`_decorate_outbound_comment`. **The Cloud comment send\n   path needs an explicit encode step that does not exist today:** unlike the\n   description path, which serializes ADF on the wire at `acli.py:205`\n   (`--description json.dumps(_text_to_adf(...))`), `acli_cli_ops.add_comment`\n   (`acli_cli_ops.py:469`) sends `--body <str>` with NO ADF encoding. Because Cloud\n   `to_wire` returns an ADF **dict**, this story adds the analogous serialize step on\n   the comment create path (`--body json.dumps(to_wire(body))`, mirroring\n   `acli.py:205`); DC's `WikiTextCodec.to_wire` returns a **str**, so the existing\n   `--body <str>` path already carries rendered wiki unchanged. To keep the local\n   comparison key aligned with the rendered wire, this story **flips the a32a codec\n   injection** (`_diff_comments`'s `_resolve_codec` argument, `outbound_comments.py`)\n   from the default `_IdentityCodec` to the real `RichTextCodec`, so the local dedup\n   key is `normalize_outbound`-consistent with what lands on Jira. Because identity\n   is now the ID map, the dedup key is only a secondary guard, so the migration\n   cannot reintroduce duplicate re-posts: an AC asserts DC comment-diff still\n   converges (zero duplicate re-post) after the injection flip (the deliberate mirror\n   of `3388`'s `test_dc_comment_diff_unchanged_by_cutover`, which pinned the key\n   UNCHANGED under the description-only cutover). The wire shape is asserted against\n   the REAL create surface, not only a fake.\n\nDescriptions + the description cutover ship first (`e59d`/`271c`/`3388`, on which\nthis story depends), so this builds on the codec-rendering + state-echo-suppression\nalready in place.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/binding_store.py` — persistent per-binding\n  comment-ID map `local_comment_key → jira_id` plus a `record_comment_id(...)` writer\n  (change-gated, append-only, fixed-shape entry).\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_comments.py` — comment differ keyed\n  by the ID map (via the local comment key) instead of the normalized body key\n  (`:143`); both builders (`_map_comments_for_create` `:148`, `_diff_comments`\n  append `:147`) add `\"local_comment_key\": <HLC>` to the mutation entry; outbound\n  create renders via `RichTextCodec.to_wire` in\n  `_map_comments_for_create`/`_decorate_outbound_comment`; flip the `_diff_comments`\n  a32a codec injection (`_resolve_codec`) from `_IdentityCodec` to the real\n  `RichTextCodec`; preserve the `RECONCILER_MARKER` machine-comment exclusion.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py` (`:418`, create path) —\n  capture `add_comment`'s returned `{\"id\": …}` and call\n  `binding_store.record_comment_id(local_comment_key, id)` (binding_store already\n  threaded here, `:154`); keep the non-fatal `comment_errors` behavior.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_apply_phases.py` (`:165`, update\n  path) — capture `add_comment`'s returned `{\"id\": …}` in\n  `_update_one_dispatch_comments` (`:147`) and record it via the threaded\n  `binding_store`.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/dispatch_one.py` (`update_one`, `:448`) — add\n  an optional `binding_store` parameter (default `None`) and forward it to\n  `_update_one_dispatch_comments`, so the update loop can reach the map.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_handlers.py` (`handle_update`, `:201`) —\n  pass the already-held `ctx.binding_store` (`BatchApplyContext.binding_store`, `:81`)\n  into `update_one`; this is the production outbound-update (batch) path.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/apply_outbound.py` (`_apply_outbound_update`,\n  def `:126`, `update_one` call `:159`) — add an optional `binding_store` param\n  forwarded from `_apply_typed` (`applier.py:447`, which already receives it) and pass\n  it through to `update_one`, so the typed-leaf caller has parity with the batch path.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira/acli_cli_ops.py` (`add_comment`,\n  `:469`) — add the Cloud comment-body encode step (`--body json.dumps(to_wire(body))`,\n  mirroring the description ADF serialize at `acli.py:205`); DC's str `to_wire` needs\n  no change.\n- `src/rebar/reducer/_processors.py` — surface the COMMENT event's stable local key\n  (the HLC `timestamp`, already on the entry at `:349`) so the differ can associate a\n  returned Jira id with the originating comment; no new per-pass state. The existing\n  inbound `jira_comment_id` field (`:354-356`) is left as-is (authoritative for\n  inbound-origin comments).\n- inbound comment mirror path (`inbound_differ._diff_comments_inbound`, `:280`) —\n  **left UNCHANGED; NOT in the edit set.** It already refuses our own outbound echoes\n  via the `RECONCILER_MARKER` body check (`:337`) and already skips Jira ids matching a\n  local `jira_comment_id` (`:299,319-321`); it does not receive and does not need the\n  outbound map. Listed here only to record that the inbound direction is deliberately\n  untouched (no cross-direction handle).\n- `tests/` — comment identity, id-capture data-path, append-only, rendering\n  (Cloud ADF encode / DC wiki), and dedup-key-migration convergence tests.\n\n## Testing\n- Duplicate bodies: two rebar comments with identical text create two distinct Jira\n  comments and map to two distinct IDs (no collision), keyed by ID not body.\n- Idempotence: a second reconcile pass creates 0 duplicate comments (mapped IDs are\n  skipped).\n- Append-only: a rebar comment edit does not push to Jira and a Jira comment edit\n  does not overwrite rebar; only genuinely new comments mirror.\n- Rich rendering: an outbound comment body is rendered through `RichTextCodec.to_wire`\n  (Cloud ADF / DC wiki), asserted by the wire shape a create receives.\n- Dedup-key migration convergence: after flipping the comment `_resolve_codec`\n  injection to the real rendering codec, DC comment-diff still converges with zero\n  duplicate re-post (the deliberate counterpart to `3388`'s\n  `test_dc_comment_diff_unchanged_by_cutover`).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Comment identity is a persistent map from a stable local comment key (the\n  COMMENT event HLC) to the Jira comment ID, not the normalized-body key; verified by\n  `test_comment_duplicate_bodies_distinct_ids` (two identical-text comments map to\n  two IDs).\n- [ ] The returned `add_comment` `{\"id\": …}` dict is captured at both enactment sites\n  and persisted against the originating comment's local key: the mutation entry\n  carries `local_comment_key` (HLC), `binding_store` reaches both loops (already in\n  `_create_one`; threaded through `update_one` and its callers `handle_update`/\n  `_apply_outbound_update` into `_update_one_dispatch_comments`), and\n  `record_comment_id` writes `local_key → id` uniformly across the Cloud (acli) and\n  DC transports; verified by `test_comment_id_captured_and_persisted` (fake client\n  returns `{\"id\": \"1\", …}`; the map records it against the local key).\n- [ ] Inbound-origin identity (the existing per-entry `jira_comment_id`, bug 85a1)\n  and the new outbound `local_key → jira_id` map are each authoritative for their own\n  direction and never compete — the inbound differ is unchanged and consults ONLY its\n  own `RECONCILER_MARKER`/`jira_comment_id` loop-breakers, never the outbound map: an\n  inbound-origin comment is never re-pushed and an outbound-origin comment is never\n  re-created; verified by `test_comment_bidirectional_identity_no_double_sync`.\n- [ ] Comment sync is append-only in both directions — new comments mirror once,\n  edits/deletes are not synced; verified by `test_comment_append_only` (an\n  edit/delete triggers no cross-side write).\n- [ ] Outbound comment bodies are rendered through the shared `RichTextCodec.to_wire`\n  seam with the Cloud comment-body ADF encode step added on the real create surface\n  (`--body json.dumps(to_wire(body))`, mirroring `acli.py:205`; DC str `to_wire`\n  unchanged), and the `_diff_comments` a32a codec injection is migrated from\n  `_IdentityCodec` to the real rendering codec so the local dedup key matches the\n  landed wire; verified by `test_comment_rendered_through_codec` (asserting the real\n  create surface's wire shape) and `test_comment_dedup_key_uses_real_codec`.\n- [ ] After the dedup-key migration, DC comment-diff converges with zero duplicate\n  re-post (the deliberate counterpart to `3388`'s comment-diff-unchanged pin);\n  verified by `test_dc_comment_diff_converges_after_migration`.\n- [ ] A repeat reconcile pass creates no duplicate comments; verified by\n  `test_comment_sync_idempotent`.\n- [ ] The comment-ID map is change-gated and adds no per-pass churn (fixed-shape,\n  one entry per comment, added once); verified by the git-delta cost harness on a\n  no-new-comment pass."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785824072208558001,"uuid":"ed4c32ba-a915-448b-bab8-b0cdf0965a68"}